--TEST--
`Core\Taint::assertTrusted` answers on the `tainted` axis alone and refuses a `secret` operand outright, so a value carrying both reveals first and asserts second — `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`, `rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The two qualifiers are independent bits (`rule:security/secret-qualifier`) and each escape hatch
// answers on one of them. `Qual::Launder` admits a `tainted` argument and no
// `secret` one — `admits_secret_argument` is `Qual::Reveal`'s alone — so the
// over-strict half of `rule:security/sink-predicate` costs a refusal here rather than a leak, and
// the composition below is the supported route rather than an ordering rule
// anything had to write down.
secret tainted string $both = "hunter2";

// The accepted order first, so a mark that stopped admitting `tainted` fails
// here as a missing refusal below rather than looking correct. Revealing
// removes `secret` and carries `tainted` through, and this is the member that
// takes it from there.
tainted string $revealed = Core\Secret::reveal($both, "the audit log records which command disclosed it");
string $trusted = Core\Taint::assertTrusted($revealed, "the value was matched against an allowlist");
echo $trusted, "\n";

// And the refusal: the other order does not compile. The operand still carries
// `secret`, which this parameter does not admit, so the diagnostic is the
// ordinary mismatch — the qualifier is part of the type, and no rule of its
// own is needed to say so.
string $wrong = Core\Taint::assertTrusted($both, "asserting says nothing about who may see it");
echo $wrong, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `secret tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:22:43
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
